};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Altar of Obedience: Trusting God Enough to Obey | DMI Calamba City"
  description="A Bible study from Philippians 2:1–11 about Christ's humility, surrendering the self-life, taking up the cross daily, and trusting God enough to obey."
  bodyClass="study-page"
>
  <header class="study-hero">
    <nav class="study-nav" aria-label="Primary navigation" data-i18n-aria-label="navAria">
      <a class="brand-lockup" href={base} aria-label="Return to DMI Calamba City home" data-i18n-aria-label="homeAria">
        <img src={logoPath} width="900" height="900" alt="" />
        <span>DMI · Calamba City</span>
      </a>
      <LanguageToggle translations={translations} />
      <span>09 August 2026</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="study-hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow" data-i18n="eyebrow">Bible Study · Philippians 2:1–11</p>
      <h1 data-i18n="title">Altar of Obedience</h1>
      <p class="study-subtitle" data-i18n="subtitle">Trusting God Enough to Obey</p>
    </div>

    <blockquote class="hero-scripture">
      <p lang="en" data-i18n="heroScripture" data-i18n-lang><sup>8</sup> “He humbled himself by becoming obedient to the point of death, even death on a cross.”</p>
      <cite lang="en" data-i18n="heroCitation" data-i18n-lang>Philippians 2:8 ESV</cite>
    </blockquote>
  </header>

  <main class="study-content">
    <aside class="study-aside" aria-label="Study summary" data-i18n-aria-label="summaryAria">
      <div>
        <span class="aside-label" data-i18n="themeLabel">Theme</span>
        <p data-i18n="theme">Surrender everything. Trust God's heart. Embrace the cross. Put your yes into action.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="aside-label" data-i18n="keyTextsLabel">Key texts</span>
        <p data-i18n="keyTexts">Philippians 1:27–30<br />Philippians 2:1–11<br />Luke 9:23</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="aside-label" data-i18n="timeLabel">Time</span>
        <p data-i18n="time">25–30 minutes</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="aside-label" data-i18n="presentationLabel">Presentation</span>
        <a class="aside-action" href="https://canva.link/jwlyr31xoxsgy5x" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" data-i18n="presentationLink">Open Canva slides <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="aside-label" data-i18n="serviceLabel">Sunday service</span>
        <a class="aside-action" href="https://www.youtube.com/live/8GgKO8HcTEg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" data-i18n="serviceLink">Watch the preaching <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <article class="study-article">
      <section class="opening" aria-labelledby="opening-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section01">01 · Begin here</p>
        <h2 id="opening-title" data-i18n="openingTitle">Will I trust God enough to obey?</h2>
        <p class="lead" data-i18n="openingLead">
          Struggles look different from one generation to another. We may hold tightly to comfort, success, control, money, relationships, titles, or reputation. But the deeper battle remains the same: <strong>Will I trust God enough to obey Him?</strong>
        </p>
        <p data-i18n="openingBody">
          The greatest battle of faith is not about ability but surrender. What often stops us is not that we cannot obey, but that we do not want to lay down what God is asking from us. Obedience may be misunderstood or go unrecognized, but human approval is not our goal. Our goal is to please God.
        </p>
        <div class="key-statement">
          <span data-i18n="keyTruthLabel">Key truth</span>
          <p data-i18n="keyTruth">Obedience will always cost us something, but what God accomplishes through it is greater than anything we surrender.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="jesus-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section02">02 · Look to Jesus</p>
        <h2 id="jesus-title" data-i18n="jesusTitle">Jesus is the picture of an obedient life</h2>
        <p data-i18n="jesusBody1">
          In Philippians 1:27–30, Paul urged believers to live worthy of the gospel—standing firm, united, and willing to suffer for Christ. The “therefore” at the beginning of chapter 2 connects that call with how they should live toward one another.
        </p>
        <p data-i18n="jesusBody2">
          In Philippians 2:5–11, Paul does more than give a beautiful description of Jesus. He presents the ultimate example of a surrendered life. Though truly God, Jesus did not use His status for His own advantage. He took the form of a servant, humbled Himself, and obeyed to the point of death on a cross.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="verse-card">
          <span data-i18n="keyVerse">Key verse · ESV</span>
          <p lang="en" data-i18n="philippiansVerse" data-i18n-lang><sup>8</sup> “He humbled himself by becoming obedient to the point of death, even death on a cross.”</p>
          <cite lang="en" data-i18n="philippiansCitation" data-i18n-lang>Philippians 2:8</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p data-i18n="jesusBody3">
          At the altar of obedience, Jesus laid down His rights, position, privileges, comfort, will, and ultimately His life. If He is our model and Lord, He also calls us to trust, surrender, and obey.
        </p>
        <div class="discussion-prompt">
          <span data-i18n="discuss1Label">Discuss · 1</span>
          <p data-i18n="discuss1">Which is hardest for me to surrender to God right now: comfort, control, success, money, a relationship, a title, or reputation?</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="center-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section03">03 · Move self from the center</p>
        <h2 id="center-title" data-i18n="centerTitle">Replace the self-life with humility</h2>
        <p data-i18n="centerBody1">
          Philippians 2:3–4 directly confronts selfish ambition and vain conceit. The issue is deeper than bad behavior. The question is: <strong>Who is at the center—self or Christ?</strong> When self is at the center, pride, self-interest, and division grow. When Christ is at the center, we learn humility, self-giving, and concern for others.
        </p>
        <p data-i18n="centerBody2">
          Humility does not deny the value God has given us. It refuses to make the self the center and lord of life. “What do I want?” is no longer the final question, but “What does Jesus want?” Not merely, “What do I deserve?” but “What does love require?”
        </p>
        <div class="practice-grid" aria-label="Contrast between the self-life and a Jesus-centered life" data-i18n-aria-label="contrastAria">
          <div>
            <span data-i18n="selfLabel">Self says</span>
            <p data-i18n="selfWant">“I want this.”</p>
            <p data-i18n="selfDeserve">“I deserve this.”</p>
            <p data-i18n="selfHappy">“I will do what makes me happy.”</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span data-i18n="jesusLabel">A follower of Jesus asks</span>
            <p data-i18n="jesusWant">“What does Jesus want?”</p>
            <p data-i18n="jesusLove">“What does love require?”</p>
            <p data-i18n="jesusFather">“What pleases the Father?”</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="discussion-prompt">
          <span data-i18n="discuss2Label">Discuss · 2</span>
          <p data-i18n="discuss2">In which decision or relationship do I need to move self from the center and consider the interests of others?</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="cross-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section04">04 · Trust</p>
        <h2 id="cross-title" data-i18n="crossTitle">Obedience demands trust</h2>
        <p data-i18n="crossBody1">
          We obey only as much as we trust. Abraham trusted God beyond reason when he offered Isaac, believing that God could raise the dead. Joseph and Daniel trusted God through betrayal, prison, and the lions' den. They obeyed when the cost was highest, and God remained faithful.
        </p>
        <p data-i18n="crossBody2">
          Jesus is the ultimate example. He obeyed the Father to the point of death—not because the cross was easy, but because He fully trusted the Father's heart. A surrendered life is a trusting life. The issue is not merely having the Holy Spirit, but being yielded to His rule.
        </p>
        <div class="key-statement dark">
          <span data-i18n="rememberLabel">Remember</span>
          <p data-i18n="remember">When we doubt God's goodness, obedience becomes difficult. When we know His character, obedience becomes worship.</p>
        </div>
        <p data-i18n="crossBody3">
          Philippians 2 shows the kingdom pattern: obedience before honor. From cross and death, Jesus moved to exaltation, lordship, and worship. We do not earn salvation through our sacrifice; we receive it through Christ's finished work. But saving grace shapes us into His cross-shaped life—surrender before glory, service before honor, and death before resurrection.
        </p>
        <div class="discussion-prompt">
          <span data-i18n="discuss3Label">Discuss · 3</span>
          <p data-i18n="discuss3">Where is God calling me to trust Him even when I cannot yet see or understand what He is doing?</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="daily-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section05">05 · Embrace the cost</p>
        <h2 id="daily-title" data-i18n="dailyTitle">Obedience may look like losing</h2>
        <blockquote class="verse-card">
          <span data-i18n="keyVerse">Key verse · ESV</span>
          <p lang="en" data-i18n="lukeVerse" data-i18n-lang><sup>23</sup> “If anyone would come after me, let him deny himself and take up his cross daily and follow me.”</p>
          <cite lang="en" data-i18n="lukeCitation" data-i18n-lang>Luke 9:23</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p data-i18n="dailyBody1">
          From a human perspective, the cross looked like failure, yet it became history's greatest victory. Obedience may require giving up ease, certainty, comfort, security, popularity, promotion, relationships, money, dreams, and control over a carefully planned future. The costs are real, but obedience is never wasted. God redeems what we place on the altar.
        </p>
        <p data-i18n="dailyBody2">
          Jesus did not say, “Merely improve yourself.” He said, “Deny yourself.” His call has three movements: <strong>deny yourself, take up your cross daily, and follow Me</strong>. He is not simply improving the old nature; He is forming us to become like Him. Every act of obedience declares, “God, I trust You more than I trust myself.”
        </p>
        <ol class="response-steps" aria-label="Three movements of daily obedience" data-i18n-aria-label="dailyStepsAria">
          <li>
            <span data-i18n="denyLabel">Deny</span>
            <p data-i18n="denyBody">Name where self is insisting on control and surrender it to the lordship of Jesus.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span data-i18n="carryLabel">Carry</span>
            <p data-i18n="carryBody">Receive the faithful and loving obedience God places before you, even when it costs.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span data-i18n="followLabel">Follow</span>
            <p data-i18n="followBody">Take the next clear step that agrees with Scripture and the character of Christ.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <div class="discussion-prompt">
          <span data-i18n="discuss4Label">Discuss · 4</span>
          <p data-i18n="discuss4">What might obedience cost me—and what clear, concrete step is God asking me to take now?</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="response-section" aria-labelledby="response-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section06">06 · Respond</p>
        <h2 id="response-title" data-i18n="responseTitle">Listen. Lay down. Obey.</h2>
        <p data-i18n="responseBody">
          In Matthew 21:28–32, Jesus tells of two sons. One refused but later obeyed; the other said “yes” but never went. God desires more than a verbal “Yes, Lord.” He desires a yes that becomes action.
        </p>
        <div class="key-statement dark">
          <span data-i18n="twoSonsLabel">Which son am I?</span>
          <p data-i18n="twoSons">The son who said yes but never went—or the son who eventually obeyed? Real obedience is more than intention; it acts.</p>
        </div>
        <p data-i18n="responseBody2">
          At the altar, ask, “What is my Isaac? What is God asking me to place on the altar?” The reward of obedience is not always immediate. Sometimes God blesses us here; sometimes He rewards us in eternity. But we never lose what we surrender to Him.
        </p>
        <ol class="response-steps">
          <li>
            <span data-i18n="listenLabel">Listen</span>
            <p data-i18n="listenBody">Open the Word and ask, “Lord, what do You want me to obey?”</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span data-i18n="layDownLabel">Lay down</span>
            <p data-i18n="layDownBody">Surrender the right, comfort, control, approval, or outcome that is resisting obedience.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span data-i18n="obeyLabel">Obey</span>
            <p data-i18n="obeyBody">Act in faith—not to be seen by people, but to please God.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="closing-prayer" aria-labelledby="prayer-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section07">07 · Pray together</p>
        <h2 id="prayer-title" data-i18n="prayerTitle">A prayer of surrender and obedience</h2>
        <p data-i18n="prayer1">
          Father, thank You that Jesus has shown us perfect humility and obedience. Forgive us for the ways we place self, comfort, control, approval, and ambition at the center.
        </p>
        <p data-i18n="prayer2">
          Give us faith to trust You enough to obey. At the altar, we surrender our Isaac and embrace our cross. Help us lay down our rights, receive Your process, and do the next thing You ask of us.
        </p>
        <p data-i18n="prayer3">
          May our lives please You and show that Jesus is Lord. In Jesus' name, Amen.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="takeaway" aria-label="Study takeaway" data-i18n-aria-label="takeawayAria">
        <span data-i18n="takeawayLabel">Take this with you</span>
        <p data-i18n="takeaway">Surrender everything. Trust God's heart. Embrace the cross. Do not merely say yes—put your obedience into action.</p>
      </section>

